<title>UK: ABB to cut thousands of jobs in Europe - paper.</title>
<headline>ABB to cut thousands of jobs in Europe - paper.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-06-09</dateline>
<text>
<p>ABB AG, the Swiss-Swedish engineering group, will axe thousands of jobs in western Europe over the next five years in an effort to boost efficiency, cut costs and spread its manufacturing operations around the world, the Financial Times said.</p>
<p>The newspaper reported in its Monday edition that the company hoped to create the same number of jobs in the emerging economies such as India, China and eastern Europe where labour costs are lower.</p>
<p>The FT said ABB's new chief executive Goran Lindhal said the group's growth would come mainly from emerging markets, adding that he had indicated that between 20,000 to 30,000 jobs would go in western Europe and North America.</p>
<p>Lindhal said maintaining the current payroll of 215,000 would depend on reaching an ambitious growth target for annual oder intake of $50 billion by 2001, compared with $36 billion last year.</p>
<p>It was not clear whether Lindhal was speaking in an interview.</p>
